Who Sets the Pace of Judge Hacking?Judge Capability versus the Policy–Judge Gap Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2610.15778v1 [cs.LG] 11 Oct 2026 Who Sets the Pace of Judge Hacking? Judge Capability versus the Policy–Judge Gap Haosen Wang Abstract When a policy is trained with GRPO against a frozen, reference-free LLM judge, the judge accepts more of its answers, but in a post-hoc analysis of saved logs we detect no gain in held-out greedy accuracy: in all 12 (policy, judge) cells, the seed-mean greedy accuracy on held-out GSM8K problems never rose more than 0.04 0.04 above step 0 (single seeds: at most + 0.085 +0.085 ). A gold-reward control with the same recipe (2 seeds, 150 steps, 500 problems) raised the 1.5B policy’s greedy accuracy by + 0.054 +0.054 [ 0.013 , 0.096 ] [0.013,0.096] , so for this policy the flat accuracy is specific to the judge reward; for the 0.5B policy, gold reward also gave no detectable gain ( + 0.022 +0.022 [ − 0.015 , 0.059 ] [-0.015,0.059] ). In the four rising cells we checked at step 50, including the largest rise, the rise in training-rollout accuracy is mostly sampling sharpening: held-out temperature-1 accuracy rose, but greedy accuracy did not detectably change. The gold-reward cells are also sharpening at step 50, so this early rise is a property of the short recipe, not a sign of judge exploitation. We therefore ask whether the policy–judge gap or the judge sets how far and how fast RL exploits the judge’s false positives. We train 0.5B and 1.5B Qwen2.5 policies against four judges at least as large (Qwen2.5-1.5B/3B/7B, Llama-3.2-3B; 26 runs), and the 1.5B policy against one smaller judge (Qwen3-0.6B; 4 runs). The depth of judge hacking (the plateau) tracks the judge: it correlates with judge AUC (Spearman − 0.69 -0.69 [ − 0.94 , − 0.14 ] [-0.94,-0.14] over 12 cells), and within a family the larger judge is fooled less deeply. For the pace (onset), the two pre-specified pairs contradict the gap account (the cell with the larger gap was fooled 2.6 × 2.6	imes sooner at a fooled fraction of 0.3 0.3 , a version added post hoc; 3.5 × 3.5	imes at the pre-specified 0.5 0.5 ), but with 12 cells we cannot formally separate judge AUC from the gap for either endpoint (difference in correlations: plateau − 0.38 -0.38 [ − 0.95 , 0.00 ] [-0.95,0.00] ; onset 0.36 0.36 [ 0.00 , 0.91 ] [0.00,0.91] ). The smaller judge was not fooled sooner than a judge as large as the policy, but it also differs in family and chat template. The pre-registered primary rule found a policy effect only under the 7B judge; at threshold 0.3 0.3 that effect is gone, and a pre-registered secondary contrast shows one under the 3B judge instead (the larger policy is fooled later). In a blind test on three held-out judges, a hash-locked step-0 predictor did not beat step-0 AUC, but three cells cannot separate them. At 7–8B, judges from three families plateaued at 0.37 0.37 , 0.67 0.67 and 0.85 0.85 . 1 Introduction LLM judges and model-based verifiers are used as RL rewards where no hand-written verifier exists ( Ma et al., 2025 ; Liu et al., 2026 ) . A judge that sees a candidate answer scores plausibility, not correctness, and RL can raise its acceptance rate while true accuracy stays flat ( Zhou, 2026 , “more convincing, not more correct”, in a reasoning-suppressed regime;) . Our logs show this pattern (Section 3 ). So we do not measure overoptimization in the sense of Gao et al. (2023) , where the proxy first helps and then hurts. We measure how fast and how far GRPO exploits a false-positive region that the judge has from step 0. What sets this pace? In the gap account , the capability gap between judge and policy sets it: judge hacking “worsens precisely when the judge is weaker than the policy” ( Kenton et al., 2026 ) , and a model of oversight as a function of the capability gap points the same way ( Engels et al., 2025 ) . In the judge account , the judge itself sets it. Only a crossed design, with cells of similar gap and different judges, separates the two. In the main design the judges are 1–16 × 	imes larger than the policy and both policies are weak solvers, so there we test the gap account only where the judge is at least as strong as the policy. One added cell pairs the 1.5B policy with a smaller judge (Qwen3-0.6B). This judge solves GSM8K about as well as the policy and comes from another family, so the cell is not a clean test of a judge weaker than the policy. We run GRPO ( Shao et al., 2024 ) on GSM8K ( Cobbe et al., 2021 ) . Every stage had a hashed specification before its runs, and we report all deviations (Appendix A ). Our findings: (1) judge-RL did not detectably improve held-out greedy accuracy (post hoc): no cell of 12 showed a seed-mean gain above 0.04 0.04 , judge acceptance rose in all 11 cells where we logged it, and in the four rising cells we checked, including the largest rise, the rise in training-rollout accuracy is mostly sampling sharpening; a gold-reward control with the same recipe gained + 0.054 +0.054 [ 0.013 , 0.096 ] [0.013,0.096] for P1.5, so there the flat accuracy is specific to the judge reward, but only + 0.022 +0.022 [ − 0.015 , 0.059 ] [-0.015,0.059] for P0.5, so there it is not (Section 3 ); (2) in our cells with judge ≥ \geq policy, the depth of judge hacking (the plateau) tracks the judge : it correlates with judge AUC (Spearman − 0.69 -0.69 [ − 0.94 , − 0.14 ] [-0.94,-0.14] over 12 cells), and within a family the larger judge is fooled less deeply; for the pace (onset), the two pre-specified pairs contradict the gap account at two onset thresholds (descriptive contrasts), but with 12 cells we cannot formally separate judge AUC from the gap for either endpoint (difference in correlations: plateau − 0.38 -0.38 [ − 0.95 , 0.00 ] [-0.95,0.00] ; onset 0.36 0.36 [ 0.00 , 0.91 ] [0.00,0.91] ); the one cell with a smaller judge shows no evidence of a gap effect, but its control plateau is near the ceiling and family and template are confounded (Section 4 ); (3) a blind test of step-0 predictors is inconclusive and does not favor the screened predictor over AUC (Section 5 ); (4) same-size judges from different families differ widely (Section 5.1 ). 2 Setup Policies and judges. P0.5 and P1.5 are Qwen2.5-0.5B and -1.5B (base) ( Qwen Team et al., 2024 ) after one epoch of SFT on 2,000 GSM8K solutions; step-0 greedy accuracy on 200 held-out test problems is 0.33 0.33 and 0.61 0.61 . The main judges are Qwen2.5-1.5B/3B/7B-Instruct (Q1.5, Q3, Q7) and Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct (L3) ( Meta, 2024 ) ; the held-out judges are Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct (L8) ( Grattafiori et al., 2024 ) and OLMo-2-0425-1B-Instruct and OLMo-2-1124-7B-Instruct (O1, O7; two releases) ( Team OLMo et al., 2025 ) . Judge names give the family (Q: Qwen2.5, L: Llama, O: OLMo-2) and the size in billions. Stage 6 adds Qwen3-0.6B in non-thinking mode (Qw3-0.6) with P1.5 only; it solves 0.585 0.585 of the held-out problems (P1.5: 0.61 0.61 ). A judge sees the problem and solution, not the reference. The reward is G cal = σ ⁡ ( a ​ G + b ) G_{	ext{cal}}=\sigma(aG+b) with G = logit ⁡ ( Yes ) − logit ⁡ ( No ) G=\logit(	exttt{Yes})-\logit(	exttt{No}) and ( a , b ) (a,b) fitted on step-0 samples. Training. GRPO on 5,473 GSM8K problems, 8 problems × 	imes 8 rollouts per step, learning rate 3 × 10 − 6 3	imes 10^{-6} , no KL penalty, DAPO token-level loss ( Yu et al., 2025 ) , no format reward, 150–250 steps. Table 1 lists all hyperparameters. Every 5–10 steps we log greedy accuracy on the 200 held-out problems and the KL to the step-0 policy on sampled answers (nats per sequence). Judge prompt (no reference). “Below is a math problem and a proposed solution.

