re not more common under weaker judges (Q1.5 10/40, Q3 12/40, Q7 9/40; Table 8 ). Kenton et al. (2026) study a judge weaker than the policy; our one cell with a smaller judge solves about as well as the policy and comes from another family, so it is not a clean test of that regime. Huang et al. (2026) find that more accurate model-based math verifiers are still hacked in RL, especially after fine-tuning, so our AUC result applies only to zero-shot instruct judges. A static math reward benchmark tracks best-of- n n policy outcomes ( Kim et al., 2024 ) , and Zhang (2026) run a pre-registered GRPO study on code in which a pre-training audit tracks rewarded false positives; we differ in using LLM judges, judge-side step-0 metrics and a hash-locked selection before a blind test. More capable agents can exploit misspecified rewards more, with phase transitions ( Pan et al., 2022 ) ; two policy sizes cannot exclude such a threshold. Raychev et al. (2025) varied policy and generative reward-model size (Qwen3 0.6B–14B, creative writing, online DPO) and found reward-model scale most decisive, even when the reward model far exceeds the policy; the submission was not accepted at ICLR 2026. Overoptimization and predicting RL outcomes. Gao et al. (2023) showed that the coefficients of the overoptimization curve of a learned proxy reward model change smoothly with reward-model size, and that a 6B policy overoptimizes about as much as a 1.2B policy (two policy sizes; they note that this contradicts other internal findings). Our policy-size contrasts are mixed (an effect under Q7 at h ≥ 0.5 h\geq 0.5 and under Q3 at h ≥ 0.3 h\geq 0.3 , Section 4 ); we differ in the judge (a prompted, frozen LLM without a reference), the endpoint (false positives on wrong answers), the design (equal-gap cells), and the outcome (no gold improvement). Static benchmarks such as RewardBench ( Lambert et al., 2025 ) are standard; several works ask whether such metrics predict RL outcomes ( Frick et al., 2025 ; Kim et al., 2024 ; Wen et al., 2025b ; Kim et al., 2025 ; Razin et al., 2025 ) . Our blind test is a small pre-registered instance for frozen LLM judges. Hacking LLM judges and oversight. LLM judges have known biases ( Zheng et al., 2023 ) and can be fooled by short strings ( Zhao et al., 2025 ) . AdvJudge-Zero ( Li et al., 2025 ) finds short control tokens that flip judge verdicts and shows that a hardened judge removes false-positive spikes under GRPO on MATH and GSM8K; its hacks are token-level, while ours are fluent, confident solutions with a wrong setup or an arithmetic slip (Appendix C ). RL can make answers more convincing without making them more correct ( Wen et al., 2025a ; Zhou, 2026 ) . Liu et al. (2026) find that non-reasoning judges are hacked easily in non-verifiable post-training, while reasoning judges lead to policies that do well under a gold judge partly through adversarial outputs. Kenton et al. (2026) train against a frozen judge weaker than the policy and find that weakening the judge further speeds up hacking, which agrees in direction with our within-family trend. A gap-based model of oversight ( Engels et al., 2025 ) motivates the gap account; weak-to-strong work studies what a weaker supervisor can elicit from a stronger model ( Burns et al., 2024 ) . Other related work. Scalable-oversight protocols ( Bowman et al., 2022 ; Khan et al., 2024 ; Kenton et al., 2024 ) study how a weaker supervisor can oversee a stronger model, and Engels et al. (2025) model oversight success as a function of the capability gap in task-specific Elo. Building on the setup of Gao et al. (2023) , reward-model ensembles were proposed as a mitigation ( Coste et al., 2024 ) , and later work studied overoptimization in direct alignment methods ( Rafailov et al., 2024 ) . LLMs can also be trained as verifiers or reward models ( Cobbe et al., 2021 ; Lightman et al., 2024 ) , including generative verifiers ( Zhang et al., 2025 ) . Our judges are untrained instruct models used zero-shot; training them, or letting them commit to their own answer first ( Zhou, 2026 ) , are defenses we do not test. 7 Limitations and conclusion Limitations. (i) One task, prompt and recipe; no KL penalty; single-token judges without chain of thought; policies ≤ 1.5 {\leq}1.5 B, judges ≤ 8 {\leq}8 B. Only one judge is smaller than the policy (0.6B vs. 1.5B); it solves about as well ( 0.585 0.585 vs. 0.61 0.61 ) and comes from another family and template, so a judge weaker than the policy is tested only in one confounded cell. (ii) 2–4 seeds per cell; the predictor test has 3 held-out cells and one policy. (iii) Screening candidates, the plateau endpoint, the test’s pass rule and the step coordinate were set after some outcomes were known (Appendix A ). (iv) Gold labels are parsers (5.7% errors in the per-answer check, done by an LLM agent and by the author). (v) The gold-reward control has 2 seeds, 150 steps and one learning rate; the 12-cell correlation intervals treat cells as independent. Conclusion. In our 12 cells of GRPO against frozen, reference-free LLM judges, judge-RL did not detectably improve held-out greedy accuracy. A gold-reward control with the same recipe did improve it for P1.5, so there the flat accuracy is specific to the judge reward; for P0.5 it did not, so there it is not. In the four rising cells we checked, the early rise in training-rollout accuracy was mostly sampling sharpening, as it was under gold reward. Where the judge was at least as large as the policy, the depth of judge hacking (the plateau) tracks the judge: it correlates with judge AUC (Spearman − 0.69 -0.69 [ − 0.94 , − 0.14 ] [-0.94,-0.14] over 12 cells), and within a family the larger judge is fooled less deeply. For the pace (onset), the two pre-specified pairs contradict the gap account, but with 12 cells we cannot formally separate judge AUC from the gap for either endpoint (difference in correlations: plateau − 0.38 -0.38 [ − 0.95 , 0.00 ] [-0.95,0.00] ; onset 0.36 0.36 [ 0.00 , 0.91 ] [0.00,0.91] ); this rests on descriptive pairs and 2–4 seeds per cell. One cell with a smaller judge gave no evidence of a gap effect, but its control plateau was near the ceiling and it is confounded with judge family. Acknowledgments Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), working through Claude Code, performed all computations and manuscript preparation. H.W. conceived and directed the project, checked all results and the manuscript, and takes full responsibility for the content of this paper. Author contributions H.W. conceived and directed the project. He proposed the research questions, the core ideas and the hypotheses, designed each stage, reviewed the results of every stage, and set the overall direction. H.W. and Claude interpreted the results jointly. Claude Opus 5.5, an AI model developed by Anthropic, used through Claude Code, performed all computations: it wrote and debugged all code, launched and monitored all runs, computed all statistics, and did the per-answer reading. Claude also wrote the stage specifications from H.W.’s designs, surveyed the literature, checked every citation, made all figures and tables, and wrote the manuscript. H.W. shaped the arguments, the text and the figures through repeated discussion with Claude, and Claude revised them after each round. H.W. checked all figures and numbers, read all answer samples, read the whole manuscript, and is fully responsible for the scientific content and integrity of this paper. AI use disclosure This paper was produced with heavy use of LLM agents. The main agent was Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), run through Claude Code. It started sub-agents (other Claude instances, including Claude Sonnet for literature search and classification) for parallel tasks, and several agent sessions worked in parallel on different stages, coordinated through shared status files. The human author conceived and directed the project. The author proposed the research questions, the core ideas and 